$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:21:Hi, I'm Andrea. Your virtual onboarding specialist with the BMH Group. Thank you for being here. The fact that you are watching this means you have made a decision to do something that matters. Not just a job. Not just a role. Something that genuinely changes people's lives
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:96:Andrea here. I'm excited to get started. Let's talk about something that matters more than any technique we'll cover later: mindset. Before you ever pick up the phone, you need the right mindset locked in. So I'm going to walk you through ten principles that shape how everyone at the BMH Group thinks and sounds on every call. These are your operating system. Get them right, and everything else gets easier.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:443:The pipeline is the full collection of active leads and deals at various stages of the process, from first contact all the way through closing. We manage the pipeline in Sandra, our CRM. Think of it like a sales funnel — lots of leads go in at the top and only a percentage make it all the way to a closed deal. The first job in any conversation is to help fill the top of that funnel.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:499:In CRM context, disposition means the outcome you record after a call. This is different from the disposition manager role we talked about earlier. After every call you make, you need to record a disposition in Sandra. Examples include not interested, no answer, callback scheduled, appointment set, do not call, and wrong number. Accurate dispositions keep the pipeline clean and make sure the right leads get the right attention. That's the vocabulary. You don't need to memorize all of it today — come back here whenever a term comes up that doesn't click yet. Up next, we'll look at the tools where all of this actually lives.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:586:For our team this tool is part of how we keep a steady pipeline of leads coming in. You need to know how it fits into our process.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1236:Now, inside each of these pipeline stages, there's a conversation happening. And every conversation you have follows a five-step framework. Step one is the Intro, where you set expectations. You tell them who you are, why you're calling, and what they'll get out of the conversation. Step two is the Fact Find, where you ask questions, qualify, and listen. This is where you should be spending about eighty percent of your conversation time. And notice I said talking about the person, not the house. Step three is the Pitch, where you present what BMH does. This is actually the only part of the conversation that's really about the property and the transaction itself. Step four is the Offer, where you transition toward next steps. For you, that usually means teeing up the handoff rather than throwing out a number.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1244:Here's the thing that ties all this together. The pipeline stages are the organizational structure. They tell you where a lead is in the overall process. The five-step framework is how you actually execute within each conversation. Pipeline stages move leads forward through the system. Conversation steps move each individual call forward.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1267:The intro is quick, simple, and to the point. Andrea will demonstrate. You've got about fifteen seconds to set the tone for the entire conversation. Fifteen seconds. That's it. Here's what a solid opening sounds like.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1271:Hi Jim, this is Andrea with the BMH Group. How are you doing today? Great. Sounds like you've got a pre-qualification for an offer and I'd like to finalize some details. When we purchase properties, we buy them as-is, we cover all closing costs, and there are no commissions involved. So by the end of this call, I'll be able to do one of two things for you. I can either give you an approval with an offer, or a denial with a reason. Did I catch you at a good time?
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1338:In the last module, you learned how to open a call and run the basic fact find. Now we're going a level deeper into discovery. And then we're going to talk about one of the most critical moments in the entire pipeline, which is the handoff. So stick with me here because this is where the real skill shows up.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1370:Alright, let's talk about the handoff. This is Stage 4 in the pipeline, and it is make-or-break. You've done discovery. You know the story. The seller is qualified, they're motivated, and they're ready to hear an offer. Now it's time to hand this lead to the acquisition team. And I cannot stress enough how much a good handoff matters. Bad handoffs kill deals that should have closed.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1963:The first call is all about discovery and qualification. Calls two and beyond are different because you're not starting from scratch anymore. You're building on a relationship that already exists, even if it's a thin one. For the second call, it sounds something like this. "Hey, this is Andrea with the BMH Group. We talked a few days ago about your property at 123 Main st. I wanted to follow up and see if you've had a chance to think about what we discussed. How are things going?" The key there is referencing the previous conversation. It shows them you remember who they are.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.md:1975:You can try a casual check-in. "Hey, it's Andrea from the BMH Group. Haven't heard from you in a bit, just wanted to make sure you're doing okay. Any updates on the property?" Or try an assumptive approach. "Hi, I wanted to let you know we're still interested in your property at 123 Main st. If the timing isn't right, no worries at all, just let me know and I'll update my notes."
