$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:619:The pipeline is the full collection of active leads and deals at various stages of the process, from first contact all the way through closing. We manage the pipeline in REsimpli. Think of it like a sales funnel — lots of leads go in at the top and only a percentage make it all the way to a closed deal. Your job as a VA is to help fill the top of that funnel.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:675:In CRM context, disposition means the outcome you record after a call. [curious] This is different from the disposition manager role we talked about earlier. After every call you make, you need to record a disposition in REsimpli. Examples include not interested, no answer, callback scheduled, appointment set, do not call, and wrong number. Accurate dispositions keep the pipeline clean and make sure the right leads get the right attention. You support the acquisition manager by finding and qualifying motivated sellers. [thoughtful] REsimpli is the hub for everything. Keep lead statuses and dispositions accurate after every call. Follow-up is where deals are made. And your KPIs are the scorecard that helps everyone, including you, get better over time.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:762:For our team this tool is part of how we keep a steady pipeline of leads coming in. You need to know how it fits into our process.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:1406:Now, inside each of these pipeline stages, there's a conversation happening. And every conversation you have follows a five-step framework. Step one is the Intro, where you set expectations. You tell them who you are, why you're calling, and what they'll get out of the conversation. [curious] Step two is the Fact Find, where you ask questions, qualify, and listen. This is where you should be spending about eighty percent of your conversation time. And notice I said talking about the person, not the house. Step three is the Pitch, where you present what BMH does. This is actually the only part of the conversation that's really about the property and the transaction itself. [pause] Step four is the Offer, where you transition toward next steps. For you, that usually means teeing up the handoff rather than throwing out a number.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:1414:Here's the thing that ties all this together. The pipeline stages are the organizational structure. [thoughtful] They tell you where a lead is in the overall process. The five-step framework is how you actually execute within each conversation. Pipeline stages move leads forward through the system. [curious] Conversation steps move each individual call forward.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:1508:In the last module, you learned how to open a call and run the basic fact find. Now we're going a level deeper into discovery. [curious] And then we're going to talk about one of the most critical moments in the entire pipeline, which is the handoff. So stick with me here because this is where the real skill shows up.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:1540:Alright, let's talk about the handoff. This is Stage 4 in the pipeline, and it is make-or-break. [serious] You've done discovery. You know the story. The seller is qualified, they're motivated, and they're ready to hear an offer. Now it's time to hand this lead to the acquisition team. And I cannot stress enough how much a good handoff matters. [emphatic] Bad handoffs kill deals that should have closed.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2187:We track six metrics, and we track them from left to right. [curious] That means we follow the lead from the moment it enters the pipeline all the way to a signed contract. Each metric feeds into the next, and when something breaks, you can trace it back to exactly where it broke.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2223:Here's what hitting your numbers looks like on a daily basis. 150 to 200 dials. 25 to 40 connections. 8 to 15 quality conversations. 3 to 5 process calls. [encouraging] And your pipeline is moving forward, meaning leads are advancing through stages or getting properly dispositioned if they're not a fit. These aren't arbitrary targets. They're the activity levels that consistently produce deals. Hit these numbers every day and the results take care of themselves. [reassuring] When a metric is off, we don't come at it like you're in trouble. We coach.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2250:When you start, you receive an hourly base rate while you're ramping up. [curious] You're learning, building skills, getting comfortable with the process. The base pay makes sure you've got income coming in while your pipeline is still developing. Once you're consistently hitting your KPIs for 30 or more consecutive days, you graduate to the full commission structure. [excited] That's the transition point.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2278:A deal gets attributed to you when you were the person who worked the lead, you completed the handoff to the acquisition team, and the deal closed with a signed contract and completed transaction. [reassuring] If a lead you worked three months ago finally closes, you still get credit. Your pipeline stays with you. [encouraging] You're not getting punished because a seller took their time.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2282:The people who earn the most here aren't necessarily the ones with the most raw volume. They're the ones who are thorough, consistent, and don't let good leads die from neglect. [thoughtful] More quality conversations means more deals in your pipeline. Consistent follow-up means those deals actually convert instead of going cold. [confident] And clean handoffs mean the acquisition team can close efficiently instead of scrambling for missing information.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2303:--- You've covered a lot of ground. Sellers, the offer, the pipeline, scripts, objections, tools, KPIs, compensation, career path, advanced situations, cold calling, FAQs. Now we're going to put all of it together into what a real day actually looks like. Because knowing the pieces is one thing. Running the whole day is something else. You clock in on HubStaff and open REsimpli. That's your command center. First thing you do is look at your follow-up list. How many callbacks are scheduled for today? What stage are they in? Who's the hottest lead, the one closest to being ready for a handoff? Sort your priorities. Stage 3 leads come first because they're closest to moving forward. Then Stage 2, the ones that still need qualification. Then Stage 1, people you haven't been able to connect with yet. For the first half hour, you open PropStream and do quick research on your top 10 to 15 leads. Verify ownership. Check property details. Look for anything useful, liens, tax status, mortgage info, anything you can reference on the call. This half hour of prep makes every conversation you have for the rest of the day better. You'll sound like you've done your homework, because you actually did. Then you start dialing. Your first calling block is the most important one. Follow-ups first, always. Start with your scheduled callbacks, the people who are expecting to hear from you at a specific time. After that, move to the other follow-up leads in your pipeline. Then new leads that haven't been contacted. You're aiming for 60 to 80 dials in this first block. After every call, you log notes in REsimpli immediately. Between dials, you fire off follow-up texts. Take a break. Seriously. Step away from the screen for fifteen minutes. Stretch. Get some water. Reset your energy. The next block needs the same energy as the first one, and it won't get it if you're running on fumes. Second calling block. Keep working your list. Mix in texts and emails between calls. If you cleared all your follow-ups in the first block, focus on new leads and re-contacts. By the time you break for lunch, you should be somewhere around 110 to 150 dials for the day. Lunch. Eat real food. Not a protein bar at your desk while scrolling your phone. After lunch, you've got an admin block. Send follow-up emails for the conversations you had that morning. Update lead stages in the CRM. If any leads are ready for handoff, complete those checklists now. Clean up any notes that need detail. Then your final calling block. This is the last push. Hit the remaining leads on your list. Try re-dials on the people who didn't pick up in the morning. Make sure every lead scheduled for today has been attempted. By the end of this block, you should be at 150 to 200 total dials. Last half hour of the day is pipeline review. Step back and look at the big picture. How many leads are sitting at each stage? Are there any stuck in Stage 1 that haven't connected after multiple attempts, maybe they need a different approach? Are there any in Stage 3 that are ripe for handoff but haven't been moved yet? Update every stage. Make sure every active lead has a next action with a specific date. No leads floating around without a plan. Then schedule your follow-ups for tomorrow. Make sure your task list in REsimpli is ready for when you sit down in the morning. Clean up anything that's stale. Clock out. You've "worked the day" when all of this is true. You hit your dial target. You completed every scheduled follow-up. You logged detailed notes on every conversation. You sent follow-up texts and emails to all active leads. You updated all lead stages that needed updating. Every active lead has a next action. Your pipeline is clean. And you clocked your full hours. If all of that is true, you worked the day. It doesn't matter if nobody picked up. It doesn't matter if you didn't close a deal. You controlled what you could control. And over time, over weeks and months, that consistency is what produces results. It's not one heroic day. It's showing up and doing it every single day.
$HOME/BMH-OS/BMH Training Course/Thinkific/_master-transcripts.backup-2026-07-01.md:2337:When you start, your job is working leads, qualifying sellers, building a pipeline, and handing off to the acquisition team. That's the foundation. [encouraging] Everything else at this company builds on top of that. Master it. Understand the sellers. Internalize the pipeline. Hit your KPIs consistently. Become the kind of person the acquisition team loves getting handoffs from, because your leads are clean, well-documented, and actually ready.
